<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="50–60 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Grammar · Lesson 7</p>
  <h1>Advice and obligation with <em>should</em>, <em>must</em>, and <em>have to</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Decide whether an action is recommended, required, prohibited, or optional. Then choose the form and register that fit: <span class="ex">You should label the boxes. Staff have to arrive by 8:30. Visitors must wear badges. You don’t have to print a ticket.</span></p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">50–60 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">recommendation · requirement · prohibition · choice</span>
    <span class="tag">should · must · have to</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Compare a suggestion, schedule, official notice, and optional task.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Choose the meaning first, then notice overlap and register.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Control modal form, have-to form, negatives, and time.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Brief a fictional event team and answer rule questions.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can recommend an action, state an ordinary or firm requirement, prohibit an action, show that an action is optional, ask about a rule, and move an obligation into past or future time.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved a modal base verb, third-person have, and a direct instruction.">
    <h3>Three checks from earlier lessons</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use the form clue and the stated purpose in each sentence.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Earlier lesson choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="can">can</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="cans">cans</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="has">has</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="have">have</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Close">Close</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Closes">Closes</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The modal takes a base verb after every subject: Marco <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="can" data-hint="A modal does not add third-person s." data-fix="Marco can translate the welcome message." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> translate the welcome message.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The singular subject Rina takes third-person present have: Rina <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="has" data-hint="The present form of have with Rina is has." data-fix="Rina has the event key." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the event key.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Give one direct instruction to stop the outside noise: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Close" data-hint="A positive imperative begins with the base verb." data-fix="Close the side door, please." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the side door, please.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice four messages for one fictional event</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Westbridge Community Center open house</h3>
    <p><b>Teammate’s suggestion:</b> <span class="ex">You should label the boxes by room. It will make setup easier.</span></p>
    <p><b>Coordinator’s schedule email:</b> <span class="ex">Staff have to arrive by 8:30 because setup begins at 8:45.</span></p>
    <p><b>Official entrance notice:</b> <span class="ex">All visitors must wear an event badge.</span></p>
    <p><b>Task list:</b> <span class="ex">Volunteers don’t have to stay after 4:00. The late team can finish cleanup.</span></p>
    <p><b>Restricted-room notice:</b> <span class="ex">Visitors must not enter the equipment room.</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which action is recommended but not required?</li>
      <li>Which two actions are required, and why might one notice sound firmer or more official?</li>
      <li>Which action is optional? Which action is prohibited?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You separated recommendation, ordinary requirement, formal firm requirement, prohibition, optional action, and register framing.">
    <h3>Discovery check: identify meaning and framing</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Meaning and framing choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="recommendation">recommendation</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="ordinary requirement">ordinary requirement</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="formal firm requirement">formal firm requirement</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="prohibited">prohibited</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="optional">optional</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the same requirement with different framing">the same requirement with different framing</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The teammate presents labeling as a useful idea, not a rule: <span class="ex">You should label the boxes</span> gives a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="recommendation" data-hint="Should presents the action as a good idea in this message." data-fix="You should label the boxes gives a recommendation." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The schedule makes 8:30 necessary in a neutral email: <span class="ex">Staff have to arrive by 8:30</span> states an <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="ordinary requirement" data-hint="Have to is the neutral everyday choice for this schedule requirement." data-fix="Staff have to arrive by 8:30 states an ordinary requirement." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The entrance sign presents badge use as official and firm: <span class="ex">Visitors must wear badges</span> states a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="formal firm requirement" data-hint="Must fits the official, firm voice chosen for the posted notice." data-fix="Visitors must wear badges states a formal firm requirement." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The restricted-room notice says visitors cannot enter: the action is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="prohibited" data-hint="Must not means there is a rule against the action." data-fix="Entering the equipment room is prohibited for visitors." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The late team can handle cleanup, so staying after 4:00 is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="optional" data-hint="Don’t have to removes necessity; it does not ban the action." data-fix="Staying after 4:00 is optional for volunteers." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span><span class="ex">We have to lock the doors at 6:00</span> in conversation and <span class="ex">Staff must lock all doors at 6:00</span> on a notice can express <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the same requirement with different framing" data-hint="Must and have to overlap; the notice chooses a firmer, more official register." data-fix="The two sentences can express the same requirement with different framing." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: choose the meaning before the form</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Do not treat these forms as three fixed steps on one strength ladder. First decide the status of the action. Is it recommended, required, prohibited, or optional? If it is required, choose how the speaker wants to frame that requirement.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>intended meaning</th><th>useful A2 choice</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>recommendation</td><td><b>should</b></td><td><span class="ex">You should label the boxes.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>advice against an action</td><td><b>shouldn’t</b></td><td><span class="ex">You shouldn’t block the exit.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>ordinary requirement in conversation</td><td><b>have to</b></td><td><span class="ex">We have to finish by six.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>firm instruction or formal requirement</td><td><b>must</b></td><td><span class="ex">Visitors must wear badges.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>prohibition</td><td><b>must not / mustn’t</b></td><td><span class="ex">Visitors must not enter.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>no necessity; the person has a choice</td><td><b>don’t / doesn’t have to</b></td><td><span class="ex">You don’t have to print a ticket.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line"><span class="ex">Must</span> and <span class="ex">have to</span> overlap. Either can describe a real obligation. In everyday U.S. conversation, <span class="ex">have to</span> is common for ordinary requirements. <span class="ex">Must</span> often sounds firmer, more formal, or more emphatic, so it is common in notices and careful instructions. Context and register matter more than a memorized “inside versus outside authority” rule.</p>
  <p>This lesson uses <span class="ex">should</span> for recommendations. <span class="ex">Should</span> has other meanings, including expectation, but those belong to a later lesson.</p>

  <h2>Build two form systems</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">Should</span> and <span class="ex">must</span> are modal verbs. They keep one form for every subject and take a base verb without <span class="ex">to</span>. <span class="ex">Have to</span> uses ordinary present and past grammar.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>purpose</th><th>should / must</th><th>have to</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>positive</td><td><span class="ex">She should call. He must sign.</span></td><td><span class="ex">I have to leave. She has to leave.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>negative</td><td><span class="ex">She shouldn’t wait. He must not enter.</span></td><td><span class="ex">I don’t have to leave. She doesn’t have to leave.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>question</td><td><span class="ex">Should I call?</span></td><td><span class="ex">Do I have to leave? Does she have to leave?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>short answer</td><td><span class="ex">Yes, you should. No, you shouldn’t.</span></td><td><span class="ex">Yes, you do. No, she doesn’t.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">Say <span class="ex">She should call</span>, not <span class="ex">she shoulds call</span>, <span class="ex">she should to call</span>, or <span class="ex">does she should call</span>. Say <span class="ex">Does she have to call?</span>, not <span class="ex">does she has to call?</span>. The word <span class="ex">does</span> already carries present-tense agreement, so the main verb returns to <span class="ex">have</span>.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You controlled invariant modal form, base verbs, have-to agreement, negatives, and question order.">
    <h3>Drill 1: complete the form system</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Modal and have-to form choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="call">call</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="to call">to call</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="must">must</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="musts">musts</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="has">has</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="have">have</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Do">Do</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Does">Does</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="doesn’t">doesn’t</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="don’t">don’t</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Should">Should</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Does should">Does should</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The modal takes the base verb without <span class="ex">to</span>: You should <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="call" data-hint="Should is followed directly by the base verb call." data-fix="You should call the coordinator." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the coordinator.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The modal does not add third-person <span class="ex">s</span>: Every visitor <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="must" data-hint="Must keeps the same form after every subject." data-fix="Every visitor must wear a badge." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> wear a badge.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The singular subject Maya takes third-person <span class="ex">has to</span>: Maya <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="has" data-hint="Have changes to has with singular Maya in the present." data-fix="Maya has to arrive by 8:30." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> to arrive by 8:30.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Ask several volunteers about the rule: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Do" data-hint="A present question with plural volunteers begins Do." data-fix="Do the volunteers have to stay after 4:00?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the volunteers have to stay after 4:00?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The badge is optional for the singular subject Lee: Lee <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="doesn’t" data-hint="A negative present statement with Lee uses doesn’t have to." data-fix="Lee doesn’t have to wear a staff badge." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> have to wear a staff badge.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Ask the coordinator for a recommendation, not a rule: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Should" data-hint="A should question moves the modal before the subject without do." data-fix="Should I label the boxes now?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> I label the boxes now?</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Keep recommendation, prohibition, and choice apart</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Three negatives, three different consequences</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">You shouldn’t use the narrow hallway.</span> The speaker recommends another route.</p>
    <p><span class="ex">You must not use the emergency exit.</span> A rule prohibits that route.</p>
    <p><span class="ex">You don’t have to use the main entrance.</span> The main entrance is optional; another entrance is available.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="note-line"><span class="ex">Must not</span> and its contraction <span class="ex">mustn’t</span> mean “do not do it.” <span class="ex">Don’t have to</span> means “it is not necessary,” so the action remains possible. <span class="ex">Shouldn’t</span> normally gives advice against an action in this lesson; it does not automatically create a rule.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You chose recommendation, advice against, ordinary requirement, formal requirement, prohibition, and lack of necessity from context.">
    <h3>Drill 2: choose the intended meaning and register</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Advice and obligation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="should">should</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="shouldn’t">shouldn’t</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="have to">have to</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="must">must</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="must not">must not</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="don’t have to">don’t have to</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>A teammate offers a useful idea, not a requirement: You <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="should" data-hint="The action is presented as a helpful recommendation." data-fix="You should put the map beside the entrance." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> put the map beside the entrance.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>A teammate advises against blocking the view, but no rule forbids it: You <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="shouldn’t" data-hint="The speaker recommends avoiding the action; this is not a formal ban." data-fix="You shouldn’t put the tall sign in front of the map." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> put the tall sign in front of the map.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>In an ordinary team update, the event schedule requires everyone to finish by six: We <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="have to" data-hint="Have to is the neutral conversational choice requested for this schedule requirement." data-fix="We have to finish cleanup by six." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> finish cleanup by six.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>An official entrance notice uses a firm formal voice: All visitors <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="must" data-hint="Must fits the firm official register requested for the notice." data-fix="All visitors must wear an event badge." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> wear an event badge.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>A posted safety rule prohibits the action: Staff <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="must not" data-hint="Must not marks a firm prohibition, not an optional action." data-fix="Staff must not block the emergency exit." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> block the emergency exit.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Digital tickets are accepted, so printing is optional: You <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="don’t have to" data-hint="No necessity means the person may print the ticket or skip it." data-fix="You don’t have to print your ticket." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> print your ticket.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Move requirements through time with <em>have to</em></h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">Must</span> can point to the present or future when context supplies the time: <span class="ex">You must sign now. You must submit the form tomorrow.</span> However, English normally uses the <span class="ex">have to</span> family when an obligation needs ordinary past, future, or other verb forms.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>time</th><th>required</th><th>not required</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>present</td><td><span class="ex">Maya has to arrive early.</span></td><td><span class="ex">Maya doesn’t have to stay late.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>finished past</td><td><span class="ex">Maya had to arrive early yesterday.</span></td><td><span class="ex">Maya didn’t have to stay late.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>future</td><td><span class="ex">Maya will have to arrive early tomorrow.</span></td><td><span class="ex">Maya won’t have to stay late.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">Do not say <span class="ex">musted</span> or <span class="ex">will must</span>. Use <span class="ex">had to</span> for a finished past requirement and <span class="ex">will have to</span> for a future requirement. <span class="ex">Didn’t have to</span> says an action was unnecessary; by itself, it does not tell us whether the person did the action anyway.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You moved neutral requirements and lack of necessity through present, past, questions, and future time.">
    <h3>Drill 3: let the time frame choose the have-to form</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Time and obligation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="has to">has to</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="had to">had to</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Did">Did</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will have to">will have to</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="won’t have to">won’t have to</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="musted">musted</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will must">will must</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The schedule requires the singular coordinator to open the center every Saturday: The coordinator <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="has to" data-hint="A present requirement with singular coordinator uses has to." data-fix="The coordinator has to open the center every Saturday." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> open the center every Saturday.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Yesterday’s power inspection created a finished past requirement: We <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="had to" data-hint="A finished past obligation uses had to, not musted." data-fix="We had to move the information table yesterday." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> move the information table yesterday.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Ask whether the finished inspection required Lee to wait: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Did" data-hint="A past have-to question begins Did and keeps have in its base form." data-fix="Did Lee have to wait for the inspector?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> Lee have to wait for the inspector?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>A new city rule begins next month, so the future requirement is not active yet: Events <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will have to" data-hint="A future obligation uses will have to, not will must." data-fix="Events will have to provide two recycling stations next month." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> provide two recycling stations next month.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The late team is scheduled next Saturday, so Maya’s early team will have no cleanup requirement: Maya <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="won’t have to" data-hint="Future lack of necessity uses won’t have to." data-fix="Maya won’t have to stay for cleanup next Saturday." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> stay for cleanup next Saturday.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Ask for advice or ask about a rule</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>information you want</th><th>natural question</th><th>answer</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>a recommendation</td><td><span class="ex">Should I move this table?</span></td><td><span class="ex">Yes, you should. It blocks the map.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>an ordinary requirement</td><td><span class="ex">Do I have to move this table?</span></td><td><span class="ex">No, you don’t. That is only a suggestion.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>a past requirement</td><td><span class="ex">Did Lee have to move it?</span></td><td><span class="ex">Yes, he did. The inspector needed the space.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line"><span class="ex">Must I...?</span> is grammatical, but it sounds formal and is uncommon in ordinary U.S. conversation. People usually ask <span class="ex">Do I have to...?</span> about a rule. In everyday U.S. speech, <span class="ex">can’t</span> and <span class="ex">not allowed to</span> are also common ways to express prohibition: <span class="ex">Visitors can’t enter. Visitors aren’t allowed to enter.</span> Formal notices often use <span class="ex">must not</span>.</p>

  <h2>Sound natural: reduce the frame and stress the action</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>In neutral speech, the important action usually carries the strongest beat. Friendly <span class="ex">should</span> is often weak /ʃəd/: <span class="ex">You should <strong>MOVE</strong> it.</span> The connected forms <span class="ex">have to</span> and <span class="ex">has to</span> commonly sound like /ˈhæftə/ and /ˈhæstə/: <span class="ex">We have to <strong>LEAVE</strong>. She has to <strong>START</strong>.</span></p>
  <p>Strong stress can change the stance, not the grammar. <span class="ex">You <strong>SHOULD</strong> move it</span> sounds corrective or more insistent. <span class="ex">You <strong>MUST</strong> leave now</span> foregrounds firm necessity. In <span class="ex">must not</span>, keep the negative meaning clear. The contraction <span class="ex">mustn’t</span> is possible, but full <span class="ex">must not</span> is common in formal U.S. notices.</p>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read recommendation, ordinary requirement, prohibition, optional action, past requirement, and rule-question listening check">
